Vote against.  There are a number of criteria which, to me, are a litmus test
for any suggested Nethack addition. This one fails them all:

1) Does it increase the tactical complexity of the game?  Nethack has become
what it is by optimising for this.  Its grandparent, Rogue, had only three
combat options (fight, run away, or use a magical item); Nethack has a
plethora (damn, always wanted to use that word!) of them, and enough diverse
situations for all to be applicable.  Unfortunately, whetstones wouldn't
provide more.  The only logical response to finding a whetstone would be to
use it until it ran out; the only effect it would have is to help the player.

2) Does it redress a general tactical imbalance in Nethack?  Many previous
posters have suggested that one exists in 3.0.  Namely, it's too hard.
Whetstones might help this; but they wouldn't help much.  You'd need a +6
whetstone just to get a +3 weapon:

>davidbe@sco.COM (The Cat in the Hat) writes:
>
>1)  They can enchant any item up to the magic level of the stone.  Thus
>    a +0 whetstone can resharpen a corroded weapon back to +0.  A +3 whetstone
>    is good to +3.  All whetstones enchant one class at a time.  To get
>    to +3 (as above) would require 3 seperate uses.
>
>5)  The + of the whetstone decreases by one each time it's used; a whetstone
>    has no charges.  This is necessary to help preserve game balance.

And a +3 weapon is no longer the sure ticket to Hell that it used to be; one
blessed enchant weapon will do this, and a serious polymorphing effort is
still necessary to win (barring inhuman patience).  The necessity of
polymorphing _is_ a serious tactical imbalance, and hopefully will be
eliminated in 3.1.  Meanwhile, there are plenty of possible improvements that
would make the game easier _and_ more tactically diverse, and hopefully the
good folks at Frobozz Magic Amulet will implement some soon.

3) Does it make the game closer to reality?  This is, in theory, a worthwhile
goal, and whetstones would surely help; but, in fact, Nethack is so far from
being a realistic "dungeon simulator" that any change, of such small magnitude
as whetstones would provide, is, in percentage terms, insignificant.  Heck,
Nethack still uses the same player-vision techniques as Rogue; it doesn't even
have a realistic system for dealing with light.  Whetstones are a long way
away.

4) Is it funny?  Not just funny when you think of it, but funny when you've
already seen it forty times?  Nethack is really good at this.  Some of its
ideas (remember Larry, Curly & Moe?) have not stood the test of time; but the
multitudes of features that exhibit Nethack's own brand of subtle, yet twisted
humor make it unique among adventure games.  Unfortunately, whetstones just
don't cut it (no pun intended).  However, if someone can manage to show me the
deep Zen, the ultimate self-parody of truth, beneath the surface of the humble
whetstone, I will retract this statement.

